Harbor: GetStorageDependencyInformation fails for volumes that aren't on a virtual disk (#92).

The stub returned ERROR_SUCCESS with NumberEntries = 0 and left the rest of the caller's buffer as it was. Excel
asks about the volume of every add-in file it loads (an XLL registered under Excel\Options\OPEN is enough) and,
after a success, reads the first entry without looking at NumberEntries: it followed the bytes that were in its
buffer as a string pointer and crashed at start. With the buffer cleared for it, it crashed on the NULL instead,
so on Windows the call can't be succeeding for an ordinary volume. Which error Windows returns has not been
checked on Windows; ERROR_INVALID_FUNCTION is what Excel was seen to accept.

Test: WindowsTests/apigaps/run.sh.

--- a/dlls/virtdisk/virtdisk_main.c	2026-11-26 16:00:03
+++ b/dlls/virtdisk/virtdisk_main.c	2026-11-26 16:02:32
@@ -40,9 +40,11 @@
     if (size < temp_size)
         return ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_BUFFER;
 
-    info->NumberEntries = 0;
-
-    return ERROR_SUCCESS;
+    /* HARBOR HACK: this returned success with no entries. Excel asks about the volume of every add-in file and,
+     * after a success, reads the first entry without looking at NumberEntries: it followed whatever was in its
+     * buffer as a string pointer, and a NULL when the buffer was cleared for it (#92). No volume here is on a
+     * virtual disk, so fail; the code Windows gives for an ordinary volume has not been checked on Windows. */
+    return ERROR_INVALID_FUNCTION;
 }
 
 DWORD WINAPI OpenVirtualDisk(VIRTUAL_STORAGE_TYPE *type, const WCHAR *path, VIRTUAL_DISK_ACCESS_MASK mask, OPEN_VIRTUAL_DISK_FLAG flags,
